For any compact Riemannian manifold $M$, we define $\curl(M)$ as the largest possible dimension of the space of sections of its tangent bundle that satisfy the Cauchy-Riemann operator derived from the metric and affine connection. Similarly, $\curl^1(M)$ represents the smallest possible codimension of the image of the Cauchy-Riemann operator on the section space of $T M\rightarrow TM$. It's evident that both $\curl(M)$ and $\curl^1(M)$ are inherent characteristics of the manifold $M$ and are invariant under diffeomorphisms. If both $\curl(M)$ and $\curl^1(M)$ are at least 1, then $M$ cannot be a K\"ahler manifold. In section 3, we find $\curl(M)$ and $\curl^1(M)$ for flat tori and products of circles with spheres in these cases: $\curl(T^{2k})=\curl^1(T^{2k})=k$, $S^1\times S^1=\begin{Bmatrix}
1
\end{Bmatrix}$, $\curl(S^3\times S^1)=\curl^1(S^3\times S^1)=0$.

The 2020 COVID pandemic's impact is stark, with measures like social distancing and lockdowns implemented globally to mitigate its spread. While effective in the short term, compliance has proven challenging. Leveraging technology, healthcare professionals can tailor treatments and provide virtual counseling to enhance patients' well-being. Building on this, we utilize the Fokker-Planck framework to analyze a customized treatment strategy for colon cancer, with the objective of maximizing immunological response. Our computational models reveal that::

(a) The targeted treatment approach effectively eradicates colon cancer and induces immune system improvement;
(b) Optimal customization varies by use case, with threshold-based customization being more efficient than threshold-dependent one for both eradicating colon cancer and inducing immune system improvement;
(c) To maximize immunological response, tailoring treatments based on threshold-dependent strategies is preferable to through threshold-based ones.
